# results/ : what each file is (generated by `scripts/06_tables.py`, `09_predictive.py`, `10_tables_more.py`)

**Status: final for the real-data files** (`datasets_computed.csv` lists the 91 data sets computed; `n_boot` in each row says the bootstrap size used: 500 in `datasets.csv`, `fragility_conclusions.csv` and `survival_curves.csv`, 300 in `matched_contrasts.csv` (joint bootstrap), 200 per replicate in `positive_control.csv`, `n_boot_real` in `se_ratio.csv`). `positive_control.csv` is complete (34 cells x 2 perturbations on each of the two graphs; `replicates_run` is the planned count). All rows are real data (`synthetic=0`) unless a file says otherwise.

Common definitions (details in `PREANALYSIS.md`): conclusion = ordered model pair (a above b) in the BT-MLE leaderboard (pair-aggregated counts, ties as half a win, ridge 1e-4). `k_upper` = smallest number of altered comparisons found by search whose exact refit puts b at or above a (a verified upper bound on the minimum; `k_lower` only where an exact/LP anchor exists). `perturbation` = `delete` (remove comparisons) or `flip` (change outcomes, non-dominated types). `z` = gap / bootstrap SE (cluster bootstrap by battle / question / game, B in `n_boot`), `z_fisher` = gap / model-based SE. `F = k_upper * g_max / SE_boot`, `Q = F / z_boot`. `Q_null`, `k_null_*` = same-graph parametric-bootstrap replicates (same pairs and counts, fitted scores, tie rate; 20 replicates) at the pair's `z_fisher`. `k_null_pred = Q_null * gap / g_max` is the k* the null predicts for this very pair without searching on the real data; `k_upper / k_null_pred = Q / Q_null`.

| file | one row per | note |
|---|---|---|
| `datasets.csv`, `datasets_computed.csv` | data set | N, models, density, tie rate, cluster unit, data version and sha256 |
| `fragility_conclusions.csv` | data set x conclusion x perturbation | 60 pairs spread over z (+ all top-1 pairs) per data set and family; `Q_null*`, `k_null*` as above |
| `fragility_by_z.csv` | data set x perturbation x z stratum ([0,1),[1,2),[2,4),[4,8),[8,inf) on `z_fisher`) | `k_med`, null: `k_null_med` (replicate pairs in the stratum), `k_null_pred_med`, `k_ratio_*` (median of k*/null-predicted k*, bootstrap over pairs) |
| `top1_conclusions.csv` | data set x perturbation | top-1 vs top-2, and the cheapest rival for the top-1 |
| `real_minus_null.csv` | data set x perturbation | Delta = mean over pairs with `z_fisher` in [1,8] of log Q - log Q_null, jackknife over models (C1, C4) |
| `matched_contrasts.csv` | contrast x member x perturbation | joint cluster bootstrap on identical battles/questions (`07_matched.py`); `paired_*` = difference to the reference member; equivalence margin 0.15 on log Q |
| `survival_curves.csv` | data set x perturbation x separation definition x subset x curve x budget | `curve`: adversarial (fraction of separated pairs with k* above the budget) or random_delete / random_flip (1 - P_flip of random alteration of the same size, interpolated from the f-grid of `02_random.py`). Every row carries the budget in four units (`b_count`, `b_se`, `b_sqrtN`, `b_frac`); `axis` says which unit the grid is regular in. `sep_def`: published (non-overlapping 95% percentile model CIs, B=500) or gap (CI of the pair's gap excludes 0). `subset`: separated, separated_top10, separated_z1_8. This file keeps `sep_def` = published, `subset` = separated and the axes `frac` and `se` (the size limit of the repository); the full grid (both separation definitions, all three subsets, the `sqrtN` axis) is written by the same script to `$BT_RESULTS/tables_full/survival_curves_full.csv`. Wilson intervals treating pairs as independent (a narrow, optimistic interval) |
| `predictive_rule.csv`, `predictive_rule_units.csv` | term / held-out fold | WLS of log k* on log z, log N, log density, tie rate; leave-one-dataset-out and leave-one-design-out |
| `predictive_references_units.csv` | held-out data set x perturbation | leave-one-data-set-out mean squared error of log k* for the reference predictors of C5 (intercept only, log z only, base model, first-order k_lin rescaled by the training median log bias, same-graph null mean k* at the pair's z raw and rescaled); `in_design_comparison` marks the held-out units of the design comparison. mtbench_gpt4 has no first-order / null reference (fewer than 5 pairs with z_fisher in [1,8]). Summaries are the `heldout_rmse_ref[...]` and `heldout_sq_err_diff(<ref>-base)[...]` rows of `predictive_rule.csv` (bootstrap over data sets) |
| `se_ratio.csv` | data set | median over pairs with z_fisher in [1,8] of resampling SE / model-based (Fisher) SE of the gap: `real_ratio_cluster_*` (cluster bootstrap, B=`n_boot_real`), `real_ratio_cmp_*` (comparison bootstrap), `null_ratio_cmp_med` (4 same-graph parametric replicates, comparison bootstrap B=200); `design_effect_cluster` = (cluster / comparison ratio)^2; `real_over_null_cmp` = real / null comparison ratio (what ties and the data's own count structure do beyond the model) |
| `positive_control.csv` | graph x departure x size x perturbation | **SYNTHETIC.** Positive control of the Delta(log Q) test (`14_positive_control.py`, `15_control_table.py`, PREANALYSIS rev 3): data simulated from the fitted BT of Arena-55k and MT-Bench human with a known departure; Delta, its replicate-mean interval, the share of replicates beyond +-0.15, `detected`, and the SE ratios / design effect of the same data. Each row is a finished cell (all replicates run); `replicates_run` is the number run, `n_replicates` those with at least 5 pairs at z_fisher in [1,8] (MT-Bench human has 15 pairs, so 1-6 of 20 replicates drop out per cell). Arena-55k: random blocks of 20 comparisons are both the unit in which the overdispersion is injected and the resampling unit; with 55 models a block holds about one comparison of a given pair, so the injected term acts almost as pair-independent noise there and the cluster SE ratio rises little (0.94 at overdispersion 2). Largest departures (overdispersion 2, position bias 2) raise Delta by about 0.02 (flip) and 0.025 (delete) over the no-departure cell; no cell reaches the margin (`detected` is False in all 68 rows).
| `synthetic_calibration.csv` | quantity x perturbation | **SYNTHETIC.** The calibration numbers of PREANALYSIS ('What the synthetic calibration says') recomputed by `16_synthetic_table.py` from `synthetic/*.parquet` and `search/tiny_exact.parquet`: exponents of log k* on log z, log N, density; mean log Q by configuration; instance sd of mean log Q (mean and maximum over configurations); k_lin/k*; P_flip RMSE and bias of the two formulas; exhaustive matches on 53 tiny problems |
| `scaling_slopes.csv` | data set x perturbation | slope of log(k*/N_sub) on log N_sub over the subsample sizes with >= 10 pairs (`17_scaling_slopes.py`, from `fragility_vs_N.csv`); interval by Monte Carlo over the point intervals, coarse; `exponent_k_vs_N` = 1 + slope |
| `random_vs_formula.csv` | data set or design pool x z range x budget | measured random-deletion reversal probability (300 draws per pair) next to Phi(-z sqrt((1-f)/f)); `fq_over_adv_*` = noise-equivalent fraction f_q (q = 16%) over the adversary's k*/N (`18_random_vs_formula.py`) |

